    13	# Design analysis for the D18 variant
    14	# -----------------------------------
    15	# a) Concrete behavior
    16	#    - The source is now always a 2x2 block with four distinct colors.
    17	#    - Direction/color binding is fixed by visible position:
    18	#        UL -> north beam, UR -> east beam, LR -> south beam, LL -> west beam.
    19	#    - Each beam is a 1-cell-wide ray emitted from its own source pixel, keeps its
    20	#      own color after turns, and still uses the legend to decide CW vs CCW turns
    21	#      when the next cell is an obstacle color.
    22	#    - Example trace: originally a 2x2 solid-8 square moving north would paint a
    23	#      2-cell-wide teal strip. Here the UL pixel alone emits north and paints a
    24	#      1-cell-wide strip in that UL color. When it sees obstacle color C, the same
    25	#      legend lookup applies, but only that 1-pixel ray turns.
    26	# b) Complexity calibration
    27	#    - Four distinct source colors are mandatory in every sample so the new rule is
    28	#      always visually present.
    29	#    - The direction mapping is fixed across samples; otherwise 2-4 examples would
    30	#      not be enough to infer it.
    31	#    - Obstacles and legend keep the original structure so only the source/ray
    32	#      semantics change.
    33	# c) Hidden assumptions from the old solver
    34	#    - Old source detection assumed one solid teal square. That fails immediately on
    35	#      a mixed-color 2x2, so source detection is replaced by a scan for the unique
    36	#      isolated 2x2 window whose four cells are pairwise distinct.
    37	#    - Old obstacle detection ignored teal in the live output because source cells
    38	#      and beam trails shared the same color. That is unsafe now: another beam's
    39	#      trail could look like an obstacle. The new solver checks obstacles against
    40	#      the immutable base grid instead of the painted output.
    41	#    - Old paint logic could harmlessly re-touch teal cells. Now recoloring a cell
    42	#      would destroy information, so painting asserts that beam paths stay disjoint.
    43	# d) Placement legality
    44	#    - Generator keeps a 1-cell moat between the 2x2 source and every obstacle.
    45	#    - Edge obstacles remain non-overlapping rectangles.
    46	#    - Span constraints still guarantee the chosen turn chains are physically
    47	#      realizable, now using 1-cell lanes instead of a sz-wide moving square.
    48	# e) Visual observability
    49	#    - Input visibly differs from the original in every instance because the source
    50	#      is never monochrome.
    51	#    - Output visibly differs because rays are no longer all the same color; all
    52	#      four source colors reappear as four beam colors.
    53	# f) Visual cleanliness
    54	#    - Beams are constrained to be non-overlapping. This avoids mixed-color knots
